---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import PeerDiagram from '../components/PeerDiagram.astro';
import { version } from '../lib/releases';
---

<Base
  toc={[
    { id: 'server-title', label: "The server, in plain terms" },
    { id: 'steps-title', label: "What happens when someone connects" },
    { id: 'password', label: "Your password on your computer" },
    { id: 'limits', label: "What this doesn’t protect against" },
  ]}
  title="Security"
  description="How Rconn connects two devices: the server introduces them, your computer checks the password with SPAKE2, and the session is encrypted end to end with DTLS-SRTP. Including what this doesn't protect against."
>
  <div class="wrap page-head">
    <h1>Who can see what</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Rconn’s server puts two devices in touch. After that, the screen, keystrokes, clipboard
      and sound go between the devices, encrypted, and the password is checked by your
      computer, not by us.
    </p>
  </div>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="server-title">
    <div class="wrap">
      <PeerDiagram />
      <h2 id="server-title" class="split-title">The server, in plain terms</h2>
      <div class="split">
        <div>
          <h3>It can</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>See which computer IDs exist and which are online.</li>
            <li>See that a device asked to connect to a computer, and when.</li>
            <li>See the network (IP) addresses it uses to introduce the two devices.</li>
            <li>Relay the encrypted stream when the devices can’t reach each other directly.</li>
            <li>Limit how many connection attempts a computer receives.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>It can’t</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Learn your access password. It never receives it.</li>
            <li>Test password guesses offline. Every guess has to be a live attempt on your computer.</li>
            <li>Read the screen, keystrokes, clipboard or sound of a session, including when it relays one.</li>
            <li>
              Put itself in the middle of a password session, even if modified to attack you: the
              password check would fail. (Sessions you accept without a password are different;
              see below.)
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="band" aria-labelledby="steps-title">
    <div class="wrap"><div class="prose">
      <h2 id="steps-title">What happens when someone connects</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Their device asks the server for your computer by its ID.</li>
        <li>
          The server introduces the two devices so they can find a network path. They set up
          an encrypted connection with DTLS, the encryption used by WebRTC. Video and sound
          travel as SRTP over it. When no direct path exists, the server relays the
          already-encrypted data.
        </li>
        <li>
          Over that connection, the two devices run a password check called SPAKE2. Each side
          proves it knows the password without sending it. The proof also covers the
          connection’s own encryption keys, so it only works on that exact connection.
          Someone who records the exchange learns nothing they can use to guess the password
          offline.
        </li>
        <li>
          No password set on your computer? Then it asks you instead: a window on your screen
          says who wants to connect, and you accept or decline. Nobody answers within 30
          seconds, and it declines.
        </li>
        <li>
          Only when the check passes, or you accept, does your computer start sending its
          screen and accepting input. Until then nothing is shown, played or accepted.
        </li>
      </ol>

      <h2 id="password">Your password on your computer</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Rconn stores a one-way check of the password (Argon2id), not the password itself,
          readable only by the computer’s administrators.
        </li>
        <li>Passwords need at least eight characters.</li>
        <li>
          After five wrong tries or declined requests, your computer refuses every attempt for a
          minute. It shows one request at a time, and at most three a minute.
        </li>
        <li>A password session reaches only that one computer.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="limits">What this doesn’t protect against</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>People you give the password to, or accept.</strong> Anyone with your ID and
          password, or anyone you accept, can see and control your computer. Only share them
          with people you trust, and change the password afterwards. Be wary of anyone who
          calls you and asks you to install remote-access software or accept a connection.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A hostile server, when you accept without a password.</strong> The devices
          exchange their encryption keys through the server. With a password, the password
          check confirms those keys, so a server that put itself in the middle would fail it.
          When you accept a connection instead, there is no shared secret to confirm them
          with: a server modified to attack you could sit in the middle of a session you
          accepted. The name in the request comes from the other device and isn’t checked
          either. Set a password, or <a href="/self-host">run your own server</a>, if that
          matters to you.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Information about connections.</strong> The server knows which devices
          connect to each other, when, and from which addresses, even though it can’t see
          what happens in the session.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Early software.</strong> Rconn {version} hasn’t had an independent security
          audit.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<style>
  .split-title { margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4rem); margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }
  .split { display: grid; gap: 2rem; }
  @media (min-width: 48rem) {
    .split { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 3rem; }
  }
  .split h3 { margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  .split ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 1.2em; }
  .split li + li { margin-top: 0.5em; }
  .prose h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
</style>
